9e9|n|A structure, usually of wood, stone, brick, or iron, erected over a river or other water course, or over a chasm, railroad, etc., to make a passageway from one bank to the other. ; Anything supported at the ends, which serves to keep some other thing from resting upon the object spanned, as in engraving, watchmaking, etc., or which forms a platform or staging over which something passes or is conveyed. ; The small arch or bar at right angles to the strings of a violin, guitar, etc., serving of raise them and transmit their vibrations to the body of the instrument. ; A device to measure the resistance of a wire or other conductor forming part of an electric circuit. ; A low wall or vertical partition in the fire chamber of a furnace, for deflecting flame, etc.; -- usually called a bridge wall.	a:281j,86,4;1w35,31,2;17s8,31,4~17s8,31,4;281j,86,4;1w35,31,2	v:281j,66,1;277h,60,9~277h,60,9;281j,66,1	V:1vkc,9,1~1vkc,9,1	n:28g5,57,6~28g5,57,6	N:ck1,77,6;1wui,63,3;1vkc,37,7;12c9,33,3;16ho,10,2~1vkc,37,7;ck1,77,6;12c9,33,3;1wui,63,3;16ho,10,2
